  "narrative": {
    "what_moves_the_lines": "Revenue is unit-count \u00d7 AUV (comps +4.5%). CoS % barely moves (73-74%) - shop-level costs scale with volume. The earnings leverage is almost entirely SG&A falling 16.0% \u2192 10.5% as corporate overhead spreads over 2.3\u00d7 the revenue \u2192 EBIT margin 9.9% \u2192 16.8%. Interest rises as debt-funded new-shop capex builds.",
    "shares_note": "No buyback authorised (thin FCF - all cash into growth). Count rises ~3%/yr from two dilutive sources: stock-based comp and Up-C (Class B/LLC-unit) conversions to Class A. So EPS growth trails NI growth by ~3pts/yr. If a buyback were introduced (not expected), each -1%/yr net share change \u2248 +1% EPS.",
    "read": "the bull/bear gap is driven far more by SG&A operating leverage and comp durability than by unit count alone. In LOW, margins never inflect and EPS only reaches $1.16 by 2029 - the PE stays stranded at ~56x (expensive for a decelerating story = de-rating risk). In HIGH, margin maturation toward CAVA-style levels compounds EPS to $3.02, taking the PE to ~21x.",
    "bottom_line": "priced for near-perfection at ~100x; BAU earnings drag the PE to ~30x by 2029 at a flat price, but LOW leaves it stranded at ~56x - that gap is the de-rating risk. The bull/bear swing is SG&A leverage + comps, not unit count. Rates barely touch earnings but dominate the multiple; oil is a double-hit (traffic + packaging/freight). No buy/sell call."
  },
  "raw_markdown": "# Dutch Bros (BROS) \u2014 PE & Earnings Model\n**Price:** $64.67 (28 Jul 2026) \u00b7 **Data:** Q1 FY2026 + FY2025 10-K \u00b7 **NYSE**\n\nBAU calibrated to management's raised FY2026 guidance ($2.05\u20132.08bn rev / $370\u2013380m adj. EBITDA). Economic EPS = total NI \u00f7 total economic shares. Up-C: FY2025 total NI $117.3m, of which $79.8m attributable to Class A.\n\n### PE from earnings \u00f7 shares (both routes converge)\n\n| Basis | NI | Shares | EPS | PE |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Reported (Class A) | $79.8m | 125.8m | $0.64 | 101x |\n| Economic (total) | $117.3m | 187.7m | $0.625 | 103x |\n\n### BAU net income bridge \u2192 shares \u2192 EPS ($m unless noted)\n\n| Line | 2025A | 2026E | 2027E | 2028E | 2029E |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| **Revenue (total)** | 1,638 | 2,055 | 2,555 | 3,133 | 3,784 |\n| \u2014 Company-operated | 1,509 | 1,918 | 2,410 | 2,980 | 3,621 |\n| \u2014 Franchise / other | 129 | 136 | 145 | 153 | 163 |\n| **Cost of sales** | 1,214 | 1,515 | 1,873 | 2,287 | 2,751 |\n| \u2014 CoS % of rev | 74.1% | 73.7% | 73.3% | 73.0% | 72.7% |\n| **SG&A** | 262 | 277 | 319 | 360 | 397 |\n| \u2014 SG&A % of rev | 16.0% | 13.5% | 12.5% | 11.5% | 10.5% |\n| **EBIT** | 162 | 263 | 363 | 486 | 636 |\n| \u2014 EBIT margin | 9.9% | 12.8% | 14.2% | 15.5% | 16.8% |\n| *(memo) +D&A* | 90 | 112 | 134 | 156 | 178 |\n| *(memo) EBITDA* | 252 | 375 | 497 | 642 | 814 |\n| Net interest | 28 | 37 | 53 | 69 | 87 |\n| Pretax | 134 | 226 | 310 | 417 | 549 |\n| Tax @16% | 21 | 36 | 50 | 67 | 88 |\n| **Net income (total)** | 113 | 190 | 260 | 350 | 461 |\n| Economic shares (m) | 188 | 193 | 199 | 205 | 211 |\n| **EPS (economic)** | **$0.60** | **$0.98** | **$1.31** | **$1.71** | **$2.18** |\n\n**What moves the lines:** Revenue is unit-count \u00d7 AUV (comps +4.5%). **CoS % barely moves** (73\u201374%) \u2014 shop-level costs scale with volume. The earnings leverage is almost entirely **SG&A falling 16.0% \u2192 10.5%** as corporate overhead spreads over 2.3\u00d7 the revenue \u2192 EBIT margin 9.9% \u2192 16.8%. Interest rises as debt-funded new-shop capex builds.\n\n**Shares:** No buyback authorised (thin FCF \u2014 all cash into growth). Count **rises ~3%/yr** from two dilutive sources: stock-based comp and Up-C (Class B/LLC-unit) conversions to Class A. So EPS growth trails NI growth by ~3pts/yr. *If* a buyback were introduced (not expected), each \u20111%/yr net share change \u2248 +1% EPS.\n\n### Low / High \u2014 what changes earnings, costs & PE (vs BAU)\n\n| Driver | LOW | BAU | HIGH | Earnings/cost effect |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| New shops/yr | 155\u2013170 | 190\u2192235 | 200\u2192270 | Fewer units \u2192 less revenue base |\n| Comps (SSS) | +2% | +4.5% | +6.5% | Weaker/stronger AUV \u2192 drops at ~55% incremental margin |\n| SG&A leverage | none (EBITDA ~18% flat) | 18\u219221% | 18\u219223.5% | **The swing factor** \u2014 no leverage keeps EBIT margin stuck ~10% |\n| Rate on debt | 8% | 6.5% | 5% | Higher interest drag on debt-funded builds |\n| **\u2192 2029 EPS** | **$1.16** | **$2.18** | **$3.02** | |\n| **\u2192 2029 PE @ $64.67** | **56x** | **30x** | **21x** | |\n\n**Read:** the bull/bear gap is driven far more by **SG&A operating leverage and comp durability** than by unit count alone. In LOW, margins never inflect and EPS only reaches $1.16 by 2029 \u2014 the PE stays stranded at ~56x (expensive for a decelerating story = de-rating risk). In HIGH, margin maturation toward CAVA-style levels compounds EPS to $3.02, taking the PE to ~21x.\n\n### Forward PE at constant $64.67 (multiple self-compresses as EPS grows)\n\n| Scenario | 2025A | 2026E | 2027E | 2028E | 2029E |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| LOW | 103 | 76 | 68 | 61 | 56 |\n| BAU | 103 | 65 | 49 | 38 | 30 |\n| HIGH | 103 | 59 | 40 | 29 | 21 |\n\n### Scenario assumptions + justification\n\n| Scenario | Shops/yr | SSS | EBITDA margin | Rate | Justification |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| LOW | ~155\u2013170 | +2% | flat 18% | 8% | Openings decelerate as East expansion cannibalises; consumer/gas headwind caps comps; no SG&A leverage; rates stay high on debt-funded builds |\n| BAU | 190\u2192235 | +4.5% | 18\u219221% | 6.5% | Guidance is \u2265185 opens + 4\u20136% comps; SG&A leverages as base scales; ties to $2.05\u20132.08bn rev / $370\u2013380m EBITDA guide |\n| HIGH | 200\u2192270 | +6.5% | 18\u219223.5% | 5% | Build machine accelerates; traffic-led comps sustain (7 straight quarters +ve transactions); margin approaches CAVA-style maturation; rates ease |\n\n### vs peers\n\n| | Trailing PE | Fwd PE | EV/EBITDA | Unit growth |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| BROS | 101x | ~65x ('26) | ~40x | 14\u201316% |\n| Starbucks | 82x | mid-20s | 15\u201318x | low-single |\n| Chipotle | 30x | 28x | 20x | ~8% |\n| Wingstop | 30\u201341x | 41\u201354x | 23\u201337x | ~17% |\n| CAVA | 150x+ | 118x | 41x | ~15% |\n\nAbove every comp except CAVA \u2014 justified only by the fastest credible EPS CAGR.\n\n### External-factor sensitivity (2027E EPS, off BAU)\n\nOil is split into **direct** (demand/traffic) and **indirect** (cost) \u2014 both attributed to oil \u2014 so it isn't double-counted with genuinely non-oil inputs. Anchor: +100bps CoS/rev \u2248 \u20118.2% EPS; \u20111.5pp SSS \u2248 \u20116.8% EPS.\n\n| Factor | Move | EPS impact | Channel |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Oil/gas \u2014 direct** | +$1/gal retail | **\u20117%** | Drive-thru traffic falls, low-income guest pulls back (\u20111.5pp SSS). Wingstop blamed gas for its Q1'26 comp miss |\n| **Oil/gas \u2014 indirect** | same move | **\u20115%** | Petrochemical packaging (cups/lids/straws) + diesel freight/distribution \u2192 ~+0.6pp CoS |\n| **Oil/gas \u2014 TOTAL** | +$1/gal | **\u2248 \u201112%** | Direct + indirect stacked |\n| Coffee + dairy (**non-oil**) | +100bps CoS/rev | \u20118% | Agricultural cycles (Brazil frost, feed/weather) \u2014 *not* oil-driven; the bulk of input cost sits here, so oil is a minority of input-cost risk |\n| Wage inflation | +100bps CoS/rev | \u20118% | Shop labour; state minimum-wage moves |\n| Rates \u2014 earnings | +100bps | \u20112.6% | Small; interest only ~2% of revenue |\n| Rates \u2014 valuation | +100bps 10Y | *multiple* | Large; ~100x long-duration equity, ~15\u201320% de-rate risk |\n\n**Note on oil vs input costs:** oil genuinely drives packaging and freight (the indirect channel above), but the two largest ingredient lines \u2014 coffee and dairy \u2014 move on their own supply cycles, so oil is *not* the majority of input-cost risk. Kept separate to avoid conflation.\n\n**One-line justification:** priced for near-perfection at ~100x; BAU earnings drag the PE to ~30x by 2029 at a flat price, but LOW leaves it stranded at ~56x \u2014 that gap is the de-rating risk. The bull/bear swing is SG&A leverage + comps, not unit count. Rates barely touch earnings but dominate the multiple; oil is a double-hit (traffic + packaging/freight). No buy/sell call.\n\n---\n*Model figures; verify against primary sources. Q2 FY2026 reports 5 Aug 2026 \u2014 refresh after.*\n",
